parts.text:
I learned to swim at the age of fifty-one, in a municipal pool in a town I shall not name, under the instruction of a woman less than half my age who addressed me throughout as 'my love'. I mention the age not for effect but because it turned out to be the whole of the difficulty. A child who cannot swim has merely not yet learned. An adult who cannot swim has, by fifty, constructed a life around the fact.

{{37}}

What ended this arrangement was not courage but a grandchild. My daughter announced that her son, then four, would be starting lessons, and asked, without malice, whether I would like to take him. I heard myself agree. It was only in the car afterwards that I understood what I had agreed to: sitting on a plastic chair, fully dressed, watching a four-year-old do something I could not.

{{38}}

There were six of us. I had expected to be the oldest and was, in fact, almost the youngest. There was a retired bus driver, two sisters in their seventies who had promised each other, and a man who had been a merchant seaman for thirty years without, as he put it, ever once getting wet on purpose.

{{39}}

The instructor's method was to refuse to discuss any of this. She was not interested in why we were afraid. She was interested in whether we could put our faces in the water and blow bubbles, and she regarded the two questions as wholly unconnected. I thought this, at first, a failure of imagination on her part.

{{40}}

Progress, when it came, did not feel like progress. There was no morning on which I woke up able to swim. There was simply a Tuesday in March when I pushed off from the wall, as I had done forty times, and failed to put my feet down.

{{41}}

I would like to report that I now swim with pleasure, and it would be almost true. I swim adequately, twice a week, in the slow lane, and I no longer think about the floor. What I had not anticipated was the effect on everything else.

{{42}}

My grandson, I should add, swims far better than I do, and has done almost from the start. He finds my technique a source of considerable amusement.

{{43}}
